# He Was Caught

An official was caught accepting a bribe and stood before the court. He pleaded that everything he received was needed for his ornamental clothing and extravagant life, that he had not saved even one lakh, and that the giver was a grand wealthy man and friend of powerful men at the Fort. Under pressure and with judgment clouded, he accepted a few green notes - not a great sum, he swore upon his children.

The rich man could dazzle seven or eight people like him. He said there was no need to die of fear because ministers were his friends and he had given not thousands but lakhs for their electoral victories. “Trust me; why this needless doubt? Take it - this is normal.” The official was fooled and enjoyed the sweetness. Now he admitted his fault, but asked the court to place the people who caused his fall in the dock and uncover the many schemes and tricks of supposedly great men.

He did not deny receiving a small bribe. But would justice seek the whole truth rather than an empty sacrificial goat? The immediate giver, Irulappan, was only an arrow. The archer was Ekambaram of Tiruppur, worth seven or eight lakhs, with Irulappan as his agent. The true giver now moved proudly among the town's rulers. The official begged the court not to fear a famous name but arrest the influential man, so justice and honesty might survive.

Ekambaram had studied with him at school and once lived by his father's lemon grove. Within a few years he mysteriously acquired lakhs and returned in luxury, greeting his old classmate Sonachalam. Sonachalam, earning three hundred rupees a month, listened open-mouthed to wonders of worldly success and proudly told his wife that the gentleman Ekambaram was his schoolmate. She urged him to remain close and learn how wealth was made.

Ekambaram promised to explain many plans when the right moment came and assured him of support through every hardship. He then asked Sonachalam to collect certain information for his business. When Sonachalam hesitated, Ekambaram mocked his cowardice, promised protection, money, reciprocal help, and even restoration of ancestral property lost to debt. Sonachalam was deceived and did wrong, but insisted that the arrested man was only the arrow and that Ekambaram must be caught.

The judge replied: the official had accepted a bribe and been caught, so he was sentenced to six years. His further allegations required evidence; without it nothing could be done. The guards were ordered to take him away. With a tormented heart, he went to prison.

---NOTES---
The poem contrasts the easy conviction of a minor corrupt official with the immunity of the wealthy political financier who engineered the crime. “Arrow and archer” is its governing image.

